  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cobalamin deficiency occurs in patients with cobalamin malabsorption when all body stores of cobalamin have been utilized and cobalamin is deficient on a cellular level.",
              "pdf_page": 372,
              "printed_page": 290,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cobalamin is required by virtually all cells in the body and plays a major role in betaoxidation, conversion of certain amino acids, and the formation of tetrahydrofolate, and is thus crucial for energy production, amino acid metabolism, and DNA and RNA synthesis.",
              "pdf_page": 372,
              "printed_page": 290,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cobalamin deficiency is associated with gastrointestinal (GI) signs and systemic complications, such as immunodeficiencies, central neuropathies, and peripheral neuropathies.",
              "pdf_page": 372,
              "printed_page": 290,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cobalamin is absorbed exclusively in the ileum of dogs and cats via a receptormediated mechanism.",
              "pdf_page": 372,
              "printed_page": 290,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The main causes of cobalamin deficiency in dogs and cats include chronic GI disease involving the ileum (e.g., inflammatory bowel disease, small intestinal dysbiosis, intestinal lymphoma), exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI), short bowel syndrome, and, in rare cases, inherited cobalamin malabsorption or dietary deficiencies.",
              "pdf_page": 372,
              "printed_page": 290,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
